INT. LAUNDRY ROOM, TWO-FLAT - EVENING

A dryer thumps. Plastic basket. Pile of small socks.

ABUELO RAÚL FONSECA (70s, cardigan, reading glasses on his forehead) sits on an upturned bucket with a cassette recorder in his lap. Fresh batteries beside it. His thumb hovers over PLAY.

He presses it.

RAÚL (V.O.)
(recorded, younger, warm)
Okay, mijos. Close your eyes. Tonight, the story of the very small crocodile.

The old man smiles at the tape. Takes a sock from the basket, matches it to its twin, folds the pair into each other. He sets it on the machine, picks up the next one, finds its mate, turns the cuff down. A third, a fourth. The dryer thumps. The tape hisses. He folds without hurry, lays each pair in a row on the lid of the washer, and when the row is full he starts another beneath it.

RAÚL (V.O.)
(recorded)
Now the crocodile had one tooth that wobbled. A big tooth. A secret tooth.

Raúl stops folding. Presses PAUSE. Looks at his own hand. Presses PLAY.

RAÚL (V.O.)
(recorded)
And he said: not today, tooth. Not today.

RAÚL
(to the recorder)
You never did finish this one.

The tape hisses. He picks up another sock.
